    "answer": "Use recursion with an index: at each step, add the current subset to the result list. Then loop from index to n - 1, choose element nums[i], recurse with i + 1, and backtrack by removing the last element.",
    "explanation": "Interview Context: Interviewers assess candidates on Backtracking to evaluate problem-solving rigor, algorithmic efficiency, and edge-case handling under the Architecture category.",
    "topic": "Backtracking",
    "category": "Architecture",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "code": "public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {\n    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n    backtrack(0, nums, new ArrayList<>(), res);\n    return res;\n}\nprivate void backtrack(int start, int[] nums, List<Integer> curr, List<List<Integer>> res) {\n    res.add(new ArrayList<>(curr));\n    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        curr.add(nums[i]);\n        backtrack(i + 1, nums, curr, res);\n        curr.remove(curr.size() - 1);\n    }\n}",
